72],[1717200000,4.87],[1719792000,6.1],[1722470400,5.47],[1725148800,4.42],[1727740800,5.3],[1730419200,5.16],[1733011200,4.24],[1735689600,5.44],[1738368000,5.48],[1740787200,4.58],[1743465600,5.7],[1746057600,5.92],[1748736000,5.24],[1751328000,6.39],[1754006400,6.97],[1756684800,6.18],[1759276800,7.08],[1761955200,9.24],[1764547200,8.27],[1767225600,9.63],[1769904000,10.2],[1772323200,8.5],[1775001600,9.59],[1777593600,9.05],[1780272000,5.87],[1782864000,2.96]]}]}],"qna":[{"question":"What is this series?","answer":"For each return period (the month of supply), the all-India total of GSTR-3B returns filed and the number of taxpayers who were eligible to file. The Total row of GSTN's state-wise summary is used. Dates are the return period, not the filing month: returns for a month are filed mostly in the following month (due around the 20th).^Counts are plain numbers of returns (about 8-10 million a month recently)."},{"question":"How should I read it?","answer":"The total filed grows slowly with the registered base. The share filed by the due date measures compliance discipline. Because the count 'filed after due date' keeps growing as late filers catch up, the most recent periods look worse than they will end up, so compare recent months only with similar vintages."},{"question":"Caveats and revisions?","answer":"The file is a snapshot 'as on' the data-considered date, so recent months are incomplete and earlier months already include late filings. Quarterly (QRMP) filers file GSTR-3B monthly or quarterly depending on scheme, which lifts the number of eligible taxpayers and filings in some months (notably the end of each quarter); a shift in scheme rules changes counts without a change in activity. The gross revenue the returns generate is not in this file."}],"related":[],"layer":"added","note":"Snapshot of filing counts as on the file date; recent return periods are not yet complete.","scraped_at":"2026-10-30T07:25:05+00:00"}